 on non‑sunny lines:\n\n* If the non‑sunny line is \\(x=1\\), the leftovers are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\); any two of them lie on a vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\) line, all non‑sunny.\n* If the non‑sunny line is \\(y=1\\), the leftovers are \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\); again any pair lies on a non‑sunny line.\n* If the non‑sunny line is \\(x+y=4\\), the leftovers are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\); each pair is on a non‑sunny line.\n\nThus covering the three remaining points would require **three** sunny lines, contradicting the requirement of exactly two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nAll logical possibilities have been exhausted:\n\n* Two sunny lines each containing two points cannot be completed by a single non‑sunny line.\n* If a sunny line contains only one required point, the non‑sunny line can cover at most three points, leaving three points that again demand three sunny lines.\n\nHence no set of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines can cover all points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, the value }k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
