.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Verification Log**\n\nBelow the solution is examined line\u2011by\u2011line. Quotations are taken verbatim from the solution; each is followed by a justification or a description of the error.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Notation  \n\n> \u201c\\(T_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\)\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  The set $T_n$ is exactly the set of required lattice points, and its cardinality is the well\u2011known triangular number $\\frac{n(n+1)}2$.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 a bound for a sunny line  \n\n> **Lemma\u202f1.**  \n> \u201cLet $L$ be a sunny line (i.e. not parallel to the $x$\u2011axis, the $y$\u2011axis or the line $x+y=0$).  \n> Then the number of points of $T_{n}$ lying on $L$ satisfies  \n> \\[\n> m(L):=|L\\cap T_{n}|\\le t,\\qquad \n> t:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*The statement of the lemma is plausible, but the proof given is not rigorous.*\n\n#### 2.1. Proof \u2013 first part (irrational slope)\n\n> \u201cWrite $L$ as $y=px+q$ with $p\\neq0,-1$ (the case of a non\u2011vertical line; the vertical case is non\u2011sunny).  \n> For $(a,b)\\in L\\cap T_{n}$ we have  \n> \\[\n> b=pa+q,\\qquad a+b=(1+p)a+q\\le n+1 .\n> \\tag{2}\n> \\]  \n> If $p$ is irrational, (2) can hold for at most one integer $a$; hence $m(L)\\le1\\le t$.\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  If $p$ is irrational and $q$ is rational, at most one integer $a$ can give an integer $b$; the argument is sound.\n\n#### 2.2. Proof \u2013 rational slope\n\n> \u201cIf $p$ is rational, write $p=\\dfrac{u}{v}$ with $\\gcd(u,v)=1$ and $v\\ge1$.  \n> From $b=pa+q$ we obtain $v b = u a + v q$; therefore $a$ must be congruent to a fixed residue modulo $v$.  \n> Consequently the admissible abscissae form an arithmetic progression with step $v$; the number of such integers satisfying (2) is at most  \n> \\[\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-q}{|1+p|\\,v}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n> \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|1+p|\\,v}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*The derivation up to this point is correct.*  The congruence condition and the arithmetic progression argument are standard.\n\n#### 2.3. Proof \u2013 the crucial inequality\n\n> \u201cBecause $p\\neq-1$, we have $|1+p|\\ge1$.  \n> Moreover $v\\ge1$ and $|1+p|\\,v\\ge2$ unless $p$ is an integer with $p\\ge1$.\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  \n- The claim \u201c$|1+p|\\ge1$ for all $p\\neq-1$\u201d is false.  For $p=-\\tfrac12$ we have $|1+p|=|1-\\tfrac12|=\\tfrac12<1$.  \n- The subsequent statement \u201c$|1+p|\\,v\\ge2$ unless $p$ is an integer $\\ge1$\u201d is also false.  Take $p=-\\tfrac12$ (so $u=-1$, $v=2$).  Then $|1+p|\\,v=\\tfrac12\\cdot2=1$, contradicting the claim.  Many rational slopes (e.g. $p=-\\tfrac13$, $v=3$) give $|1+p|\\,v=1$.\n\nBecause this inequality is used to replace the denominator $|1+p|\\,v$ by $2$, the bound\n\\[\nm(L)\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\nis **not justified** by the argument presented.  The proof therefore contains a **justification gap** (the bound may still be true, but it has not been proved).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Necessary condition for $k$\n\n> \u201cSuppose a configuration contains exactly $k$ sunny lines and $n-k$ non\u2011sunny lines.  \n> A non\u2011sunny line (vertical, horizontal or of slope $-1$) can contain at most $n$ points of $T_{n}$.  \n> Using Lemma\u202f1 for the sunny lines we obtain  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{n(n+1)}2 =|T_{n}|\n> \\le (n-k)\\,n + k\\,t .\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*The statement that a non\u2011sunny line contains at most $n$ points is correct (vertical line $x=i$ contains $n+1-i\\le n$ points, etc.).*  \n\n*However, inequality (3) relies on Lemma\u202f1.  Since Lemma\u202f1 has not been proved rigorously, the derived necessary condition is not firmly established.*\n\n> \u201cSubstituting $t=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor$ and solving (3) for $k$ gives  \n> \\[\n> k\\le\n> \\begin{cases}\n> n-1, & n\\ \\text{even},\\\\[1mm]\n> n,   & n\\ \\text{odd}.\n> \\end{cases}\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Algebraic manipulation from (3) to (4) is correct **provided** (3) holds.*\n\n---\n\n### 4. Reduction to a smaller triangle  \n\n> \u201cFix an integer $k$ satisfying (4) and put $r:=k$.  \n> Use the $n-r$ vertical lines  \n> \\[\n> V_{i}:\\;x=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n-r)\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]  \n> (all non\u2011sunny).  \n> These lines cover every point of $T_{n}$ whose abscissa is at most $n-r$.  \n> The uncovered points are exactly  \n> \\[\n> \\{(n-r+j,\\;b)\\mid 1\\le j\\le r,\\;1\\le b\\le r+1-j\\},\n> \\]  \n> i.e. a translate of the set $T_{r}$.\u201d\n\n*All statements in this paragraph are correct.  The reduction is a standard \u201ccut off a rectangle\u201d argument.*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Sub\u2011problem: covering $T_{r}$ with $r$ sunny lines  \n\n> \u201c**Sub\u2011problem.** For any positive integer $r$ there exist $r$ distinct sunny lines covering all points of $T_{r}$.\u201d\n\n*This is the crucial claim that must be proved.  The solution attempts a construction using Hall\u2019s marriage theorem.*\n\n#### 5.1. Definition of the lines  \n\n> \u201cFor each $i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,r\\}$ define the line  \n> \\[\n> S_{i}:\\; y = i\\,x + c_{i},\n> \\qquad \n> c_{i}:=1-i\\,(r+1-i).\n> \\tag{6}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*The definition is explicit, but the subsequent use of these lines is erroneous.*\n\n#### 5.2. Claim about a specific point  \n\n> \u201cThe point $(r+1-i,\\,i)$ belongs to $S_{i}$ because  \n> \\[\n> i\\,(r+1-i)+c_{i}=i\\,(r+1-i)+1-i\\,(r+1-i)=1 .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  Substituting $x=r+1-i$ into $y=i x + c_i$ yields $y=1$, **not** $y=i$.  Hence the point that lies on $S_i$ is $(r+1-i,1)$, not $(r+1-i,i)$.  The intended \u201ctopmost point of the column\u201d is therefore **not** on $S_i$.\n\n#### 5.3. Consequence drawn from the (false) claim  \n\n> \u201cConsequently each line $S_{i}$ meets the column $x=r+1-i$ of the triangle $T_{r}$ in the **topmost** point of that column.\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  Because the previous claim is false, the line actually meets the **bottommost** point $(r+1-i,1)$.  Thus the construction does **not** place a sunny line through the topmost point of each column, and consequently does **not** cover the whole set $T_{r}$.\n\n#### 5.4. Bipartite graph description  \n\n> \u201cLet $A$ be the set of points of $T_{r}$ and let $B=\\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{r}\\}$.  \n> Connect a point $P\\in A$ to a line $S_{i}\\in B$ if $P$ lies on $S_{i}$.  \n> Because the slope of each $S_{i}$ is positive, a line meets each column of the triangle in at most one point; hence the degree of every vertex of $B$ is at most $r$ and the degree of a vertex of $A$ is at most $1$.\u201d\n\n*The statement \u201cdegree of a vertex of $A$ is at most $1$\u201d is **incorrect** for the intended covering.  In the actual construction (with the erroneous $c_i$) many points of $T_{r}$ have **degree\u202f0** because they are not incident to any $S_i$ at all (e.g. the point $(1,r)$).  Even if the correct $c_i$ were used, each point would still lie on at most one line, but many points would lie on none, so the degree\u2011$0$ issue remains.*\n\n#### 5.5. Hall\u2019s condition argument  \n\n> \u201cTake any subset $U\\subseteq A$.  \n> Let $c$ be the smallest abscissa among the points of $U$.  \n> All points of $U$ lie in columns whose abscissae are at least $c$; therefore they can be incident only with the lines $S_{i}$ whose topmost point lies in a column $\\ge c$, i.e. with at most $r-c+1$ lines.  \n> On the other hand, the number of points of $U$ does not exceed the total number of points in the columns $c,c+1,\\dots ,r$, which equals  \n> \\[\n> \\sum_{j=c}^{r}(r+1-j)=\\frac{(r-c+1)(r-c+2)}2 .\n> \\]  \n> One checks directly that  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{(r-c+1)(r-c+2)}2\\le r-c+1\\qquad\\text{for all }c,\n> \\]  \n> which is equivalent to $(r-c+2)\\le2$, i.e. $c\\ge r$.  \n> Hence the only possible violation of Hall\u2019s condition would be when $c=r$; in that case $U$ consists of a single point $(r,1)$ and the neighbour set is $\\{S_{1}\\}$, so the condition still holds.  \n> Therefore every subset $U\\subseteq A$ has at least $|U|$ neighbours in $B$.\u201d\n\n**Critical Errors.**  \n\n1. The inequality $\\frac{(r-c+1)(r-c+2)}2\\le r-c+1$ is **false** for most $c$.  For example, with $r=5$ and $c=1$ the left\u2011hand side equals $\\frac{5\\cdot6}{2}=15$, while the right\u2011hand side is $5$.  \n\n2. The argument assumes that every point of $A$ has at least one neighbour in $B$ (i.e. lies on some $S_i$).  As shown in \u00a75.2\u20135.3, many points (e.g. $(1,r)$) are not on any $S_i$, so the neighbour set of a non\u2011empty $U$ can be empty, violating Hall\u2019s condition outright.\n\nBecause Hall\u2019s condition is not satisfied, the claimed existence of a perfect matching is **unproved**; in fact the constructed family of lines does **not** cover $T_{r}$ at all.\n\n#### 5.6. Conclusion of the sub\u2011problem  \n\n> \u201cConsequently the $r$ sunny lines $S_{1},\\dots ,S_{r}$ together cover **all** points of $T_{r}$.\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  The preceding construction fails to cover all points; the statement is false.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Putting everything together  \n\n> \u201cFor a given admissible $k$ (i.e. satisfying (4)) we use the $n-k$ vertical lines (5) and the $k$ sunny lines $S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}$ described in (6) (translated by the vector $(n-k,0)$ so that they act on the uncovered translate of $T_{k}$).  \n> The resulting family consists of exactly $n$ distinct lines, exactly $k$ of which are sunny, and every point of $T_{n}$ lies on at least one of them.\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  This step depends on the (false) claim that the $k$ sunny lines $S_i$ cover the translate of $T_{k}$.  Since the sub\u2011problem was not solved, the construction does **not** guarantee coverage of all points of $T_{n}$.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Extremal cases  \n\n> \u201c* $k=0$.  Take the $n$ vertical lines $x=1,\\dots ,x=n$.  \n> * $k=n$ when $n$ is odd.  The construction above uses no vertical lines (the \u201cvertical part\u201d is empty) and the $n$ sunny lines $S_{1},\\dots ,S_{n}$ cover $T_{n}$ directly.  \n> * $k=n$ when $n$ is even is impossible because (4) forbids it; the inequality (3) becomes strict in this case.\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  The claim that the $n$ sunny lines $S_{1},\\dots ,S_{n}$ cover $T_{n}$ for odd $n$ rests on the flawed construction of \u00a75. Consequently the existence of a configuration with $k=n$ (odd $n$) is **not established**.\n\n---\n\n### Overall assessment  \n\nThe solution correctly identifies a necessary bound on $k$ (inequality\u202f(4)), but the proof of Lemma\u202f1 is incomplete, and the sufficiency part (construction for any admissible $k$) collapses due to a **critical error** in the definition of the sunny lines $S_i$ and a **critical error** in the Hall\u2011matching argument.  Because the central construction does not actually cover the required set of points, the claim that *all* $k$ satisfying (4) are attainable is unproved.  Hence the solution is **invalid**."

