            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is even:}&k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd:}&k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper bound for a sunny line.**  \n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Then  \n   \\[\n   |L\\cap T_{n}|\\le s_{n}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write the line as \\(y=px+q\\) with \\(p\\neq0,-1\\) (the vertical case is non‑sunny).  \n   For a lattice point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\cap L\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b=pa+q,\\qquad a+b=(1+p)a+q\\le n+1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(p\\) is irrational, (2) can hold for at most one integer \\(a\\); hence (1) is true.  \n\n   Assume \\(p\\) rational and write it in lowest terms \\(p=u/v\\) with \\(v\\ge1\\) and \\(\\gcd(u,v)=1\\).  \n   From \\(b=pa+q\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   v b = u a + v q .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Equation (3) forces the abscissae of the lattice points on \\(L\\) to lie in a single residue class\n   modulo \\(v\\); consequently they form an arithmetic progression  \n\n   \\[\n   a=a_{0}+tv,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (4) into the inequality in (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (a_{0}+tv)(1+u/v)\\le n+1-q .\n   \\]\n\n   Multiplying by \\(v\\) and using \\(|1+u/v|=|u+v|/v\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   (a_{0}+tv)\\,|u+v|\\le v\\,(n+1-q) .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the admissible integers \\(t\\) form a finite interval whose length is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{v\\,(n+1-q)}{|u+v|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|u+v|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the line is sunny we have \\(u+v\\neq0\\).  \n   Moreover \\(|u+v|\\ge2\\):  \n   * if \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) have the same sign then \\(|u+v|\\ge u+v\\ge2\\);  \n   * if they have opposite signs then \\(|u+v|=|\\,|v|-|u|\\,|\\ge1\\), and the equality \\(|u+v|=1\\) would give\n     either \\(u=0\\) (slope \\(0\\), non‑sunny) or \\(v=1,\\;u=-2\\) (slope \\(-2\\)), but for \\(|u+v|=1\\) the\n     coefficient \\(|1+p|=|1+u/v|=|u+v|/v\\) equals \\(1/v\\le1\\) and (2) forces at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) integer\n     solutions; a direct check shows that the bound (1) still holds.  \n\n   Consequently \\(|u+v|\\ge2\\) and (6) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   |L\\cap T_{n}|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   This proves Lemma 1. ∎\n\n3. **Necessary restriction on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   A non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   Using Lemma 1 for the \\(k\\) sunny lines we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_{n}|\n   \\le (n-k)\\,n + k\\,s_{n}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(n=2t\\) (even) or \\(n=2t+1\\) (odd).\n\n   *If \\(n=2t\\) (even):* \\(s_{n}=t\\).  Inequality (7) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   2t^{2}+t\\le (2t-k)2t + k t =4t^{2}-k t .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(2t^{2}+t\\le4t^{2}-k t\\), i.e. \\(k\\le2t-1=n-1\\).\n\n   *If \\(n=2t+1\\) (odd):* \\(s_{n}=t+1\\).  Inequality (7) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   (2t+1)(t+1)\\le (2t+1-k)(2t+1)+k(t+1)\n   =4t^{2}+4t+1-kt .\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying gives \\(k\\le2t+1=n\\).\n\n   Thus we have proved the **necessary condition**\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n4. **A family of sunny lines that covers a whole triangle.**  \n\n   *Lemma 2.*  \n   For every positive integer \\(m\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain all points of \\(T_{m}\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   – **Case \\(m\\) odd.**  \n     Write \\(m=2r+1\\).  \n     For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define  \n\n     \\[\n     L_{i}:\\; y = x + c_{i},\\qquad \n     c_{i}=m+1-2i .\n     \\tag{9}\n     \\]\n\n     The slope of each \\(L_{i}\\) is \\(1\\); therefore every \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny and the lines are distinct because the intercepts \\(c_{i}\\) are distinct.  \n\n     Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{m}\\).  Set  \n\n     \\[\n     i:=\\frac{m+1-(b-a)}{2}.\n     \\tag{10}\n     \\]\n\n     Since \\(a,b\\) are positive integers and \\(a+b\\le m+1\\), the numbers \\(a+b\\) and \\(b-a\\) have the same parity; because \\(m+1\\) is even, the right‑hand side of (10) is an integer.  \n     Moreover \\(1\\le i\\le m\\) because \\(-(m-1)\\le b-a\\le m-1\\).  Substituting \\(i\\) into (9) gives  \n\n     \\[\n     b = a + (m+1-2i) ,\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{i}\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_{m}\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(\\{L_{i}\\}\\). ∎\n\n   – **Case \\(m\\) even.**  \n     Write \\(m=2r\\).  \n     First apply the odd‑case construction to the triangle \\(T_{m-1}=T_{2r-1}\\); we obtain \\(m-1\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) covering all points of \\(T_{m-1}\\).  \n     The only points of \\(T_{m}\\) not yet covered are those with abscissa \\(m\\); because \\(a+b\\le m+1\\) this column contains the single point \\((m,1)\\).  \n     Add the line  \n\n     \\[\n     L_{m}:\\; y = 2x - (2m-1) ,\n     \\tag{11}\n     \\]\n\n     which passes through \\((m,1)\\) and has slope \\(2\\) (sunny).  The set \\(\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{m}\\}\\) therefore covers all of \\(T_{m}\\). ∎\n\n5. **Construction for the original problem.**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy the necessary condition (8).  \n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny vertical lines.*  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{j}:\\; x=j\\qquad (1\\le j\\le n-k).\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are distinct, non‑sunny and together cover every point of \\(T_{n}\\) whose\n   abscissa does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining triangle.*  \n   After removing the covered columns, the uncovered points are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(n-k+i,\\;b)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-i\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. a translate by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\) of the set \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – sunny covering of the translate.*  \n   Apply Lemma 2 to the triangle \\(T_{k}\\).  \n   If \\(k\\) is odd, we obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\); after translating them by \\((n-k,0)\\) we get \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the uncovered part.  \n   If \\(k\\) is even, Lemma 2 gives \\(k-1\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) covering \\(T_{k-1}\\) and a further sunny line of slope \\(2\\) covering the single point \\((k,1)\\); after the same translation we again have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the remaining points.\n\n   All together we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n6. **Extremal cases.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n\n   *\\(k=n\\) with \\(n\\) odd.*  The construction of Lemma 2 with \\(m=n\\) (all lines of slope 1) already provides the required family; no vertical lines are needed.  \n\n   *\\(k=n\\) with