e slope \\(1\\) (sunny) and distinct intercepts, hence are distinct.  \n     Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{m}\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le m+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge1\\), the numbers \\(a+b\\) and \\(b-a\\) have the same parity; since \\(m+1\\) is even,  \n\n     \\[\n     i:=\\frac{m+1-(b-a)}{2}\n     \\]\n\n     is an integer.  Moreover \\(- (m-1)\\le b-a\\le m-1\\) gives \\(1\\le i\\le m\\).  \n     Substituting this \\(i\\) into (7) yields \\(b=a+(m+1-2i)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{i}\\).  \n     Hence every point of \\(T_{m}\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(\\{L_{i}\\}\\). ∎\n\n   – **\\(m\\) even, \\(m=2r\\).**  \n     Apply the odd‑case construction to \\(T_{m-1}=T_{2r-1}\\); we obtain \\(m-1\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) covering all points of \\(T_{m-1}\\).  \n     The only point of \\(T_{m}\\) not yet covered is \\((m,1)\\).  \n     Add the line  \n\n     \\[\n     L_{m}:\\;y=2x-(2m-1),                                   \\tag{8}\n     \\]\n\n     which passes through \\((m,1)\\) and has slope \\(2\\) (sunny).  \n     The resulting \\(m\\) sunny lines cover all of \\(T_{m}\\). ∎\n\n5. **Construction for the original problem.**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy the necessary condition (6).  \n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny vertical lines.*  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{j}:\\;x=j\\qquad (1\\le j\\le n-k).                     \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   They are distinct, non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_{n}\\) whose abscissa does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining triangle.*  \n   After removing the points covered by the vertical lines, the uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   U:=\\{(n-k+i,\\;b)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-i\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   This is exactly the translate by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\) of the set \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – sunny covering of the translate.*  \n   Apply Lemma 2 to \\(T_{k}\\).  \n   - If \\(k\\) is odd, Lemma 2 gives \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\); translating each line by \\((n-k,0)\\) yields \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(U\\).  \n   - If \\(k\\) is even, Lemma 2 provides \\(k-1\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) covering \\(T_{k-1}\\) and the extra line (8) covering the single point \\((k,1)\\).  Translating the whole family by \\((n-k,0)\\) again gives exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(U\\).\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) falls into one of the two categories:\n\n   1. **\\(a\\le n-k\\).** Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (9).  \n   2. **\\(a> n-k\\).** Write \\(a=n-k+i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  \n      Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b\\le k+1-i\\).  \n      Hence \\((a,b)=(n-k+i,b)\\) belongs to the translated set \\(U\\) and, by construction of Step 3, lies on one of the sunny lines.  \n\n   Thus each point of \\(T_{n}\\) is on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and all lines are distinct.\n\n6. **Extremal cases.**  \n\n   - **\\(k=0\\).** Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   - **\\(k=n\\) with \\(n\\) odd.** Lemma 2 with \\(m=n\\) supplies \\(n\\) sunny lines (all of slope 1); no vertical lines are needed.  \n   - **\\(k=n\\) with \\(n\\) even.** This is impossible because (6) forbids it; the counting inequality (5) is strict for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n   Consequently the bound (6) is both necessary and sufficient.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion** – The admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly those described in (6), i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is even:}&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd:}&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]"
