            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is even:}&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd:}&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Maximum points on a sunny line (Lemma 1).**  \n   Write a non‑vertical sunny line as \\(y=px+q\\) with \\(p\\neq0,-1\\).  \n   Put \\(p=u/v\\) in lowest terms (\\(v\\ge1,\\; \\gcd(u,v)=1\\)).  \n   Integer points on the line satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   a=a_{0}+tv,\\qquad b=b_{0}+ut\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb Z)          \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   for a unique pair \\((a_{0},b_{0})\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b=(a_{0}+b_{0})+(u+v)t .                                 \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the line is sunny, \\(u+v\\neq0\\).  \n   From (2) we see that the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(|u+v|\\) when \\(t\\) changes by \\(1\\).  \n   The conditions \\(1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) give the two independent bounds  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\le\\frac{n-a_{0}}{v},\\qquad \n   t\\le\\frac{n+1-(a_{0}+b_{0})}{|u+v|}.                     \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the number of admissible integers \\(t\\) (and therefore the number of points of \\(T_{n}\\) on the line) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   1+\\min\\!\\left\\{\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-a_{0}}{v}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n                     \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-(a_{0}+b_{0})}{|u+v|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\right\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(v\\ge1\\) and \\(|u+v|\\ge1\\), the right‑hand side never exceeds \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   The only delicate situation is \\(|u+v|=1\\).  \n   In this case \\(v\\) must be \\(1\\) or \\(2\\) (the possibilities are \\((u,v)=(-2,1)\\) giving slope \\(-2\\) and \\((u,v)=(-1,2)\\) giving slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \n   For \\((u,v)=(-2,1)\\) we have \\(a\\) increasing by \\(1\\) and \\(b\\) decreasing by \\(2\\); the bound from the inequality \\(b\\ge1\\) forces at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.  \n   For \\((u,v)=(-1,2)\\) we have \\(a\\) increasing by \\(2\\) and \\(b\\) decreasing by \\(1\\); the bound from \\(a\\le n\\) again yields at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.  \n\n   Thus **every sunny line contains at most**  \n\n   \\[\n   s_{n}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_{n}\\). ∎\n\n3. **Necessary condition on \\(k\\).**  \n   A non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   Using Lemma 1 for the \\(k\\) sunny lines we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)\\,n+k\\,s_{n}.                \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(n=2t\\) (even) or \\(n=2t+1\\) (odd).\n\n   *If \\(n=2t\\):* \\(s_{n}=t\\).  Inequality (5) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   2t^{2}+t\\le (2t-k)2t+kt=4t^{2}-kt,\n   \\]\n   i.e. \\(k\\le2t-1=n-1\\).\n\n   *If \\(n=2t+1\\):* \\(s_{n}=t+1\\).  Inequality (5) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   (2t+1)(t+1)\\le (2t+1-k)(2t+1)+k(t+1)=4t^{2}+4t+1-kt,\n   \\]\n   i.e. \\(k\\le2t+1=n\\).\n\n   Hence a **necessary condition** is  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\begin{cases}\n   n-1,&n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\n   n,  &n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}                                            \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n4. **A family of sunny lines covering a whole triangle (Lemma 2).**  \n\n   *Lemma 2.* For every integer \\(m\\ge1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_{m}\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n\n   – **\\(m\\) odd, \\(m=2r+1\\).**  \n     For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define  \n\n     \\[\n     L_{i}:\\;y=x+(m+1-2i).                                   \\tag{7}\n     \\]\n\n     All lines have slope \\(1\\) (sunny) and distinct intercepts, hence are distinct.  \n     Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{m}\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le m+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge1\\), the numbers \\(a+b\\) and \\(b-a\\) have the same parity; since \\(m+1\\) is even,  \n\n     \\[\n     i:=\\frac{m+1-(b-a)}{2}\n     \\]\n\n     is an integer.  Moreover \\(- (m-1)\\le b-a\\le m-1\\) gives \\(1\\le i\\le m\\).  \n     Substituting this \\(i\\) into (7) yields \\(b=a+(m+1-2i)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{i}\\).  \n     Hence every point of \\(T_{m}\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(\\{L_{i}\\}\\). ∎\n\n   – **\\(m\\) even, \\(m=2r\\).**  \n     Apply the odd‑case construction to \\(T_{m-1}=T_{2r-1}\\); we obtain \\(m-1\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) covering all points of \\(T_{m-1}\\).  \n     The only point of \\(T_{m}\\) not yet covered is \\((m,1)\\).  \n     Add the line  \n\n     \\[\n     L_{m}:\\;y=2x-(2m-1),                                   \\tag{8}\n     \\]\n\n     which passes through \\((m,1)\\) and has slope \\(2\\) (sunny).  \n     The resulting \\(m\\) sunny lines cover all of \\(T_{m}\\). ∎\n\n5. **Construction for the original problem.**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy the necessary condition (6).  \n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny vertical lines.*  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{j}:\\;x=j\\qquad (1\\le j\\le n-k).                     \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   They are distinct, non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_{n}\\) whose abscissa does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining triangle.*  \n   After removing the points covered by the vertical lines, the uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   U:=\\{(n-k+i,\\;b)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-i\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   This is exactly the translate by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\) of the set \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – sunny covering of the translate.*  \n   Apply Lemma 2 to \\(T_{k}\\).  \n   - If \\(k\\) is odd, Lemma 2 gives \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\); translating each line by \\((n-k,0)\\) yields \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(U\\).  \n   - If \\(k\\) is even, Lemma 2 provides \\(k-1\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) covering \\(T_{k-1}\\) and the extra line (8) covering the single point \\((k,1)\\).  Translating the whole family by \\((n-k,0)\\) again gives exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(U\\).\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) falls into one of the two categories:\n\n   1. **\\(a\\le n-k\\).** Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (9).  \n   2. **\\(a> n-k\\).** Write \\(a=n-k+i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  \n      Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b\\le k+1-i\\).  \n      Hence \\((a,b)=(n-k+i,b)\\) belongs to the translated set \\(U\\) and, by construction of Step 3, lies on one of the sunny lines.  \n\n   Thus each point of \\(T_{n}\\) is on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and all lines are distinct.\n\n6. **Extremal cases.**  \n\n   - **\\(k=0\\).** Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   - **\\(k=n\\) with \\(n\\) odd.** Lemma 2 with \\(m=n\\) supplies \\(n\\) sunny lines (all of slope 1); no vertical lines are needed.  \n   - **\\(k=n\\) with \\(n\\) even.** This is impossible because (6) forbids it; the counting inequality (5) is strict for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n   Consequently the bound (6) is both necessary and sufficient.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion** – The admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly those described in (6), i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is even:}&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd:}&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
