nd can only *increase* the claimed maximum, so the final inequality is not rigorously justified.\n\nThe claim that the expression never exceeds \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) is plausible (the worst case occurs when \\(v=1\\) and \\(|u+v|=1\\)), but the proof does not examine all possibilities nor does it show that the bound cannot be exceeded when negative \\(t\\) are allowed.  Consequently the argument leaves a **justification gap**.\n\n*Conclusion:* Lemma 1’s statement is true, but the proof is incomplete; a gap in justification is present.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Inequality (5) – Necessary condition  \n\n> “A non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n>  Using Lemma 1 for the \\(k\\) sunny lines we obtain  \n>  \\[\n>  |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)\\,n+k\\,s_{n}.                \\tag{5}\n>  \\]”\n\n**Analysis.**  \nFor a vertical line \\(x=c\\) the admissible points are \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); the maximum occurs at \\(c=1\\) and equals \\(n\\).  The same bound holds for a horizontal line and for a line of slope \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=d\\) contains exactly \\(d-1\\le n\\) points).  Hence the bound “at most \\(n\\) points” is correct.\n\nSubstituting the bound from Lemma 1 (\\(s_n=\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\)) yields inequality (5).  Assuming Lemma 1 is true, (5) follows logically.  No error here.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Lemma 2 – Existence of \\(m\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_m\\)\n\n#### 4.1 Odd \\(m\\)  \n\n> “For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define  \n>  \\[\n>  L_{i}:\\;y=x+(m+1-2i).                                   \\tag{7}\n>  \\]  \n>  …  \n>  Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{m}\\). … the numbers \\(a+b\\) and \\(b-a\\) have the same parity; since \\(m+1\\) is even,  \n>  \\[\n>  i:=\\frac{m+1-(b-a)}{2}\n>  \\]  \n>  is an integer … Substituting this \\(i\\) into (7) yields \\(b=a+(m+1-2i)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{i}\\).”\n\n**Analysis.**  \nThe construction uses only lines of slope \\(+1\\), which are indeed sunny.  However, the parity argument is flawed.  For a point \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\) we have \\(a+b\\le m+1\\).  While it is true that \\(a+b\\) and \\(b-a\\) have the same parity (because \\((a+b)+(b-a)=2b\\) is even), this does **not** guarantee that \\(m+1-(b-a)\\) is even.  Since \\(m+1\\) is even (because \\(m\\) is odd), \\(m+1-(b-a)\\) is even **iff** \\(b-a\\) is even.  When \\(b-a\\) is odd, the expression \\(\\frac{m+1-(b-a)}{2}\\) is a half‑integer, not an integer, so the claimed index \\(i\\) does not exist.\n\nA concrete counter‑example: take \\(m=3\\).  The point \\((1,2)\\in T_3\\) has \\(b-a=1\\) (odd).  The three lines given by (7) are\n\\[\ny=x+2,\\; y=x,\\; y=x-2,\n\\]\nnone of which passes through \\((1,2)\\).  Hence the construction fails to cover all points of \\(T_3\\).\n\nTherefore the statement “for every odd \\(m\\) there exist \\(m\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_m\\)” is **false**.  This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n#### 4.2 Even \\(m\\)  \n\n> “Apply the odd‑case construction to \\(T_{m-1}=T_{2r-1}\\); we obtain \\(m-1\\) sunny lines … Add the line  \n>  \\[\n>  L_{m}:\\;y=2x-(2m-1).                                   \\tag{8}\n>  \\]”\n\n**Analysis.**  \nThe even‑case construction explicitly depends on the odd‑case construction for \\(T_{m-1}\\).  Since the odd‑case result is false, the even‑case argument collapses as well.  Consequently the claim that “for every even \\(m\\) there exist \\(m\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_m\\)” is also **false**, constituting another **Critical Error** (though it follows from the previous one).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Step 5 – Construction for the original problem  \n\n> “Let \\(k\\) satisfy the necessary condition (6).  \n>  …  \n>  *Step 3 – sunny covering of the translate.*  \n>  Apply Lemma 2 to \\(T_{k}\\). …”\n\n**Analysis.**  \nBecause Lemma 2 is false, the proposed covering of the set  \n\\[\nU:=\\{(n-k+i,\\;b)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-i\\}