            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\;\\text{or}\\;k\\ \\text{odd},\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3,5,\\dots ,n\\} &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,3,5,\\dots ,n-1\\} &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_{m}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_{m}|=\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Maximum points on a sunny line (Lemma 1).**  \n   A sunny line has equation \\(y=px+q\\) with rational slope\n   \\(p=u/v\\;(v>0,\\; \\gcd(u,v)=1)\\) and \\(u\\neq0,\\;u\\neq -v\\).  \n   Integer points on the line are\n   \\[\n   (a,b)=(a_{0}+tv,\\;b_{0}+tu),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Hence the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by \\((u+v)t\\).  \n   Because the line is sunny, \\(u+v\\neq0\\); therefore the sum changes by at\n   least \\(\\pm1\\) when \\(t\\) varies by \\(\\pm1\\).  \n   Inside the triangle \\(T_{m}\\) we have the two independent bounds  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le a_{0}+tv\\le m,\\qquad 1\\le b_{0}+tu,\\qquad \n   a_{0}+b_{0}+(u+v)t\\le m+1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   From (2) we obtain\n   \\[\n   t_{\\max }:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m-a_{0}}{v}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad\n   t_{\\min }:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{1-a_{0}}{v}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\n   \\]\n   and similarly for the bound coming from the sum condition.\n   The number of admissible integers \\(t\\) is therefore at most  \n\n   \\[\n   1+\\min\\!\\Bigl\\{\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m-a_{0}}{v}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n                     \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1-(a_{0}+b_{0})}{|u+v|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(v\\ge1\\) and \\(|u+v|\\ge1\\), the right‑hand side never exceeds\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   Moreover the bound \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) is attained\n   by the two families  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\ell^{(1)}_{c}:\\; y=-2x+c ,\\qquad c=m+2,\\\\\n   &\\ell^{(2)}_{c}:\\; a+2b=c ,\\qquad c=m+2,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   each of which contains exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n   points of \\(T_{m}\\).  \n   Consequently\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line meets }T_{m}\\text{ in at most }\n          s_{m}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\text{ points}.}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A necessary condition for \\(k\\).**  \n   In a solution we have \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n   A non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at\n   most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  Using (5) we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   =|T_{n}|\n   \\le (n-k)\\,n+k\\,s_{n}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(n=2t\\) (even) or \\(n=2t+1\\) (odd).  \n   Because \\(s_{n}=t\\) for even \\(n\\) and \\(s_{n}=t+1\\) for odd \\(n\\), (6)\n   becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   2t^{2}+t\\le 4t^{2}-kt &\\Longrightarrow\\ k\\le 2t-1=n-1,\n   &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\\\\[2mm]\n   (2t+1)(t+1)\\le (2t+1)^{2}-kt &\\Longrightarrow\\ k\\le 2t+1=n,\n   &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a **necessary** condition is 