integers  \n\n   \\[\n   (2t+2-r),\\;(2t+1-r),\\dots ,(t+2-r).\n   \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   ---\n   **Points on a line of type \\(x+2y=D\\).**  \n   The integer solutions are  \n\n   \\[\n   (x,y)=(D-2j,\\;j),\\qquad j\\in\\mathbb Z .\n   \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n\n   Inside \\(T_{k}\\) we need \\(1\\le j\\le t+1\\) and\n   \\(1\\le D-2j\\le t+1-j\\).  For the values\n   \\(D=2t+3+r\\;(0\\le r\\le t-1)\\) the admissible integers are  \n\n   \\[\n   j=1,2,\\dots ,t+1-r .\n   \\tag{17}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently each line \\(L_{2r+2}\\) also contains exactly \\(t+1\\) points,\n   whose sums are  \n\n   \\[\n   (t+2+r),\\;(t+3+r),\\dots ,(2t+2+r).\n   \\tag{18}\n   \\]\n\n   ---\n   **The last line \\(L_{2t+1}\\).**  \n   Here \\(D=2t+4\\).  Equation (16) gives the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (x,y)=(2t+4-2j,\\;j),\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,t+1 .\n   \\tag{19}\n   \\]\n\n   Their sums are exactly the integers  \n\n   \\[\n   (t+3),\\;(t+4),\\dots ,(2t+3),\n   \\tag{20}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the remaining \\(t+1\\) sums that have not appeared in (15) or (18).\n\n   ---\n   **Partition.**  \n   The three families (15), (18) and (20) together list each integer\n   \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le 2t+2\\) exactly once; for a fixed sum \\(s\\) there is\n   exactly one point of \\(T_{k}\\) with that sum lying on the corresponding\n   line.  Hence the \\(k=2t+1\\) lines in (11)–(12) are pairwise disjoint and\n   their union is the whole set \\(T_{k}\\).  ∎\n\n5. **Construction for the original problem.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy the necessary condition (10).  \n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny vertical lines.*  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{j}:\\;x=j\\qquad (1\\le j\\le n-k).\n   \\tag{21}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover every point of \\(T_{n}\\) whose abscissa does not exceed\n   \\(n-k\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining triangle.*  \n   After removing the points covered by the vertical lines, the uncovered\n   set is  \n\n   \\[\n   U:=\\{(n-k+i,\\;b)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\l