cases}\n   n-1 & (n\\text{ even}),\\\\\n   n   & (n\\text{ odd}).\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, (4) applied to the smaller triangle \\(T_{k}\\) gives\n   \\[\n   k\\,s_{k}\\ge |T_{k}|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(s_{k}=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), inequality (9) forces\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\); i.e. **\\(k\\) must be odd**\n   (or \\(k=0\\)).  Combining with (8) we obtain the necessary condition\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd},\\;k\\le n,\\;\n          \\text{and if }n\\text{ is even then }k\\le n-1.}\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Lemma 2 (Covering \\(T_{k}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, \\(k\\) odd).**  \n   Let \\(k=2t+1\\) with \\(t\\ge0\\).  Define the following \\(k\\) lines.\n\n   *For \\(r=0,1,\\dots ,t-1\\)*\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{2r+1}&:\\; y=-2x+(2t+3-r),\\\\[1mm]\n   L_{2r+2}&:\\; x+2y=(2t+3+r).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   *The last line*\n   \\[\n   L_{2t+1}:\\; x+2y=3t+3 .\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   All constants are integers; the slopes are \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\), so each\n   line is sunny.  The constants in (11) are pairwise distinct, and the\n   constant in (12) equals \\(3t+3\\), which does not appear among the\n   constants of (11); consequently the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\n\n   **Verification that they cover \\(T_{k}\\).**  \n   Any integer point of \\(T_{k}\\) satisfies \\(2\\le a+b\\le 2t+2\\).  Set\n   \\[\n   s:=a+b\\qquad (2\\le s\\le 2t+2).\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   *Case 1: \\(s\\le t+2\\).*  \n   Write \\(r:=t+2-s\\) (so \\(0\\le r\\le t-1\\)).  Then \\(s=2t+3-r-(t+1)\\).  \n   Consider the line \\(L_{2r+1}: y=-2x+(2t+3-r)\\).  \n   Solving the system\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   y=-2x+(2t+3-r),\\\\\n   x+y=s,\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   gives \\(x=t+1-r\\) and \\(y=s-(t+1-r)=t+2-r\\), both positive integers.\n   Hence \\((x,y)\\in T_{k}\\) and lies on \\(L_{2r+1}\\).\n\n   *Case 2: \\(s\\ge t+3\\).*  \n   Write \\(r:=s-(t+3)\\) (so \\(0\\le r\\le t-1\\)).  Then \\(s=2t+3+r-(t+1)\\).  \n   Consider the line \\(L_{2r+2}: x+2y=2t+3+r\\).  \n   Solving together with \\(x+y=s\\) yields\n   \\[\n   y=t+2+r,\\qquad x=s-y=t+1-r,\n   \\]\n   which are again positive integers satisfying \\(x+y=s\\).  Thus the point\n   belongs to \\(L_{2r+2}\\).\n\n   *Case 3: the remaining sum \\(s=t+3\\) when \\(r=0\\) in the previous\n   description is already covered; the only sum not appearing in Cases 1\n   and 2 is \\(s=t+3\\) when \\(r=t\\).  This sum is handled by the last line\n   \\(L_{2t+1}\\): solving\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   x+2y=3t+3,\\\\\n   x+y=t+3,\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   gives \\(y=t+1,\\;x=t+2\\), a point of \\(T_{k}\\) on \\(L_{2t+1}\\).\n\n   The three families are mutually disjoint because each family corresponds\n   to a different value of \\(s\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_{k}\\) lies on\n   exactly one of the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\).  ∎\n\n5. **Construction for the original problem (sufficiency).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy the necessary condition (10).\n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny vertical lines.*  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines\n   \\[\n   V_{j}:\\;x=j\\qquad (1\\le j\\le n-k).\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n   These lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining triangle.*  \n   Points of \\(T_{n}\\) whose abscissa exceeds \\(n-k\\) form the set\n   \\[\n   U:=\\{(n-k+i,\\;b)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-i\\}.\n   \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n   The map \\((x,y)\\mapsto (x-(n-k),y)\\) translates \\(U\\) onto the triangle\n   \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – sunny covering of the translate.*  \n   Apply Lemma 2 to \\(T_{k}\\).  Translating each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines\n   \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) obtained there by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\) yields\n   \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines\n   \\[\n   \\widetilde L_{i}:\\;L_{i}\\text{ shifted right by }n-k.\n   \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n   Because translation preserves incidences, the family\n   \\(\\{\\widetilde L_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) covers exactly the set \\(U\\).\n\n   *Verification of full coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n   - If \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) from\n     (14).\n   - If \\(a>n-k\\) write \\(a=n-k+i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  \n     From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le k+1-i\\); thus \\((a,b)\\in U\\) and,\n     by construction of Step 3, belongs to one of the lines \\(\\widetilde\n     L_{i}\\).\n\n   Hence every required point is covered by at least one of the \\(n\\) lines,\n   exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and all lines are distinct.\n\n6. **Extremal cases.**  \n\n   - \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   - If \\(n\\) is odd, \\(k=n\\) is allowed: Lemma 2 with \\(k=n\\) provides a\n     family of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering all points, so no vertical lines\n     are needed.  \n   - If \\(n\\) is even, \\(k=n\\) is impossible because it violates the\n     necessary bound (8); the inequality in (6) is strict for \\(k=n\\).\n\n   Therefore the condition (10) is both necessary and sufficient.\n\n**Conclusion** – The admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly those described in\nthe boxed answer above."
