            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is even:}&k\\in\\{0,1,3,5,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd:}&k\\in\\{0,1,3,5,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\nIn words: \\(k=0\\) is always possible; otherwise \\(k\\) must be odd and not larger than \\(n\\), and when \\(n\\) is even the value \\(k=n\\) is excluded (because it is even).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_{m}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_{m}|=\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (Maximum points of a sunny line).**  \n   A sunny line has equation \\(y=px+q\\) with rational slope \\(p=u/v\\) where\n   \\(\\gcd(u,v)=1,\\;v>0,\\;u\\neq0,\\;u\\neq -v\\).  \n   All integer points on the line are\n   \\[\n   (a,b)=(a_{0}+tv,\\;b_{0}+tu),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   for some particular solution \\((a_{0},b_{0})\\).  \n   The sum \\(a+b\\) changes by \\((u+v)t\\).  \n   Because the line is sunny, \\(u+v\\neq0\\); hence \\(|u+v|\\ge1\\).  \n   Inside the triangle \\(T_{m}\\) we must have\n   \\[\n   1\\le a_{0}+tv\\le m,\\qquad\n   1\\le b_{0}+tu\\le m,\\qquad\n   a_{0}+b_{0}+(u+v)t\\le m+1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   From the first two inequalities we obtain\n   \\[\n   t_{\\min }^{(x)}:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{1-a_{0}}{v}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\qquad\n   t_{\\max }^{(x)}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m-a_{0}}{v}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n   \\]\n   and similarly from the \\(y\\)‑coordinate\n   \\[\n   t_{\\min }^{(y)}:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{1-b_{0}}{u}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\qquad\n   t_{\\max }^{(y)}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m-b_{0}}{u}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   From the sum condition we obtain\n   \\[\n   t_{\\min }^{(s)}:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2-(a_{0}+b_{0})}{u+v}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\qquad\n   t_{\\max }^{(s)}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1-(a_{0}+b_{0})}{u+v}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   The admissible integers \\(t\\) are those belonging to the intersection of the four intervals\n   \\([t_{\\min }^{(x)},t_{\\max }^{(x)}]\\), \\([t_{\\min }^{(y)},t_{\\max }^{(y)}]\\) and\n   \\([t_{\\min }^{(s)},t_{\\max }^{(s)}]\\).  \n   Consequently the number of admissible \\(t\\) is at most\n   \\[\n   1+\\min\\!\\Bigl\\{t_{\\max }^{(x)}-t_{\\min }^{(x)},\n                 t_{\\max }^{(y)}-t_{\\min }^{(y)},\n                 t_{\\max }^{(s)}-t_{\\min }^{(s)}\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(v\\ge1\\) and \\(|u+v|\\ge1\\), each of the three differences in (3) is\n   bounded above by \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  Hence\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line meets }T_{m}\\text{ in at most }\n          s_{m}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\text{ points}.}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The bound is sharp: the two families  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell^{(1)}_{c}:\\;y=-2x+c,\\qquad\n   \\ell^{(2)}_{c}:\\;x+2y=c,\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   with \\(c=m+2\\) each contain exactly \\(s_{m}\\) points of \\(T_{m}\\).\n\n3. **Necessary condition for \\(k\\).**  \n   In a feasible configuration we have \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\n   lines (vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   A non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) (its\n   intersection with