{m}{|u+v|}.\n   \\]\n   Because \\(|v|\\ge1,\\;|u|\\ge1\\) and \\(|u+v|\\ge1\\) (the last inequality follows from\n   \\(u\\neq -v\\)), the number of admissible integers \\(t\\) is at most\n   \\[\n   1+\\min\\!\\Bigl\\{\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{|v|}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n                 \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{|u|}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n                 \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{|u+v|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n   \\le 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line meets }T_m\\text{ in at most }\n          s_m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\text{ points}.}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The bound is sharp; for example the families  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell^{(1)}_c:\\;2x+y=c,\\qquad \n   \\ell^{(2)}_c:\\;x+2y=c,\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   with \\(c=m+2\\) each contain exactly \\(s_m\\) points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n3. **Necessary condition for \\(k\\).**  \n   In a feasible configuration we have \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\n   lines (vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   A non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (its intersection\n   with the strip \\(1\\le x\\le n\\)).  \n   Using (3) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)\\,n+k\\,s_n .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(n=2t\\) (even) or \\(n=2t+1\\) (odd).  \n   Because \\(s_n=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\),\n\n   *if \\(n=2t\\):* (3) gives \\(t\\bigl(2t+1\\bigr)\\le 4t^{2}-kt\\), i.e. \\(k\\le 2t-1=n-1\\);\n   *if \\(n=2t+1\\):* we obtain \\((2t+1)(t+1)\\le (2t+1)^{2}-kt\\), i.e. \\(k\\le 2t+1=n\\).\n\n   Thus  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1 & (n\\text{ even}),\\\\\n   n   & (n\\text{ odd}).\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, applying (3) to the smaller triangle \\(T_k\\) gives\n   \\[\n   k\\,s_k\\ge |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(s_k=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), (7) forces\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\); i.e. **\\(k\\) is odd** (or\n   \\(k=0\\)).  Combining with (6) we obtain the *necessary* condition  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd},\\;k\\le n,\\;\n          \\text{and if }n\\text{ is even then }k\\le n-1.}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Covering the triangle \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (odd \\(k\\)).**  \n   Let \\(k=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge0\\)).  \n   Define the following \\(k\\) lines:\n\n   *For \\(r=0,1,\\dots ,t-1\\)*\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   A_r &: \\;2x+y = 2t+3-r ,\\\\[1mm]\n   B_r &: \\;x+2y = 2t+3+r .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   *The last line*\n   \\[\n   D :\\; x-y = 0 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   All three families have slopes \\(-2,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\;1\\); none is parallel to the\n   \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), hence every line is sunny.\n   The constants in (9) are pairwise distinct, therefore the \\(k\\) lines are\n   distinct.\n\n   **Lemma 4.1.** *Each line \\(A_r\\) and each line \\(B_r\\) contains exactly\n   \\(t+1\\) points of \\(T_k\\); the line \\(D\\) also contains exactly \\(t+1\\) points\n   (the points with \\(a=b\\)).*  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   For a fixed \\(r\\) the equation \\(2x+y=c\\) with \\(c=2t+3-r\\) together with\n   \\(x+y\\le 2t+2\\) yields\n   \\[\n   x\\ge c-(2t+2)=1-r,\\qquad\n   x\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{c-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor = t+1 .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(0\\le r\\le t-1\\), the admissible integers \\(x\\) are\n   \\(1+r,2+r,\\dots ,t+1\\); there are exactly \\(t+1\\) of them, and each gives a\n   unique integer \\(y=c-2x\\ge1\\).  Hence \\(|A_r\\cap T_k|=t+1\\).  \n   The same computation with the symmetric equation \\(x+2y=c\\) proves\n   \\(|B_r\\cap T_k|=t+1\\).  \n   For \\(D\\) we have \\(x=y\\) and the condition \\(2x\\le 2t+2\\); thus\n   \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,t+1\\), i.e. \\(|D\\cap T_k|=t+1\\). ∎\n\n   **Lemma 4.2 (Coverage).** *Every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on one of the lines\n   listed in (9)–(10).*  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) and put \\(s=a+b\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le 2t+2\\)).\n\n   *Case 1: \\(a=b\\).*  \n   Then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(D\\).\n\n   *Case 2: \\(a>b\\).*  \n   Write \\(r:=a-b-1\\).  Because \\(a>b\\) we have \\(r\\ge0\\); the inequality\n   \\(a+b\\le 2t+2\\) gives \\(a\\le t+1+r\\), whence \\(r\\le t-1\\).  Consequently the\n   line \\(A_r\\) is defined.  Substituting \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) into the equation of\n   \\(A_r\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   2a+b = a+(a+b)=a+s = (t+2+r)+(2t+3-r)- (t+2)=2t+3-r ,\n   \\]\n   i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(2x+y=2t+3-r\\); thus \\((a,b)\\in A_r\\).\n\n   *Case 3: \\(b>a\\).*  \n   This is symmetric to Case 2.  Put \\(r:=b-a-1\\) (\\(0\\le r\\le t-1\\)).  Then\n   \\[\n   a+2b = b+(a+b)=b+s = (t+2+r)+(2t+3+r)- (t+2)=2t+3+r ,\n   \\]\n   which is exactly the defining equation of \\(B_r\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in B_r\\).\n\n   All possibilities are exhausted, so every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on at least one\n   of the lines \\(A_0,\\dots ,A_{t-1},B_0,\\dots ,B_{t-1},D\\). ∎\n\n   Lemmas 4.1 and 4.2 show that the \\(k\\) sunny lines listed above cover the\n   whole set \\(T_k\\) and each of them contains the maximal possible number\n   \\(s_k=t+1\\) of points.  Consequently the bound (3) is attained and the\n   construction is optimal.\n\n5. **Construction for the original problem.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy the necessary condition (8).  \n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny vertical lines.*  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (1\\le j\\le n-k).\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n   These lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining triangle.*  \n   Points of \\(T_n\\) whose abscissa exceeds \\(n-k\\) form the set\n   \\[\n   U:=\\{(n-k+i,\\;b)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-i\\}.\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n   The affine map \\((x,y)\\mapsto (x-(n-k),y)\\) translates \\(U\\) onto the\n   triangle \\(T_k\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – sunny covering of the