+y=t\\;(2\\le t\\le n+1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Reformulation\n\nChoose subsets  \n\n\\[\nA\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\nB\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\nC\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsider the family of non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F(A,B,C)=\\{x=r\\mid r\\in A\\}\\cup\\{y=s\\mid s\\in B\\}\\cup\\{x+y=t\\mid t\\in C\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F(A,B,C)\\) **iff**\n\n\\[\na\\in A\\;\\text{or}\\;b\\in B\\;\\text{or}\\;a+b\\in C. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following combinatorial covering problem:\n\n> Find the smallest possible value of  \n> \\[\n> s:=|A|+|B|+|C|\n> \\]\n> such that (5) holds for every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\nIf the minimum is \\(s_{\\min}\\), then we can take exactly \\(s_{\\min}\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the ones described by (4)) and fill the remaining \\(n-s_{\\min}\\) slots with any distinct sunny lines; the covering property is unchanged.  Hence the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\nk=n-s_{\\min},\\;n-s_{\\min}+1,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nBecause we are required to have **exactly** \\(k\\) sunny lines, the maximal possible \\(k\\) equals \\(n-s_{\\min}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A lower bound for \\(s\\)\n\nDefine the anti\u2011diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD:=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\subseteq S_n .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(D\\) have the same sum \\(i+(n+1-i)=n+1\\).\n\n*If the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (i.e. \\(n+1\\in C\\)) is **not** used*, then each point of \\(D\\) must be covered by a vertical line \\(x=i\\) or a horizontal line \\(y=n+1-i\\) (by (5)).  \nSince the \\(n\\) points of \\(D\\) have pairwise distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates and also pairwise distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, covering them without the diagonal line forces at least \\(n\\) distinct vertical/horizontal lines.  Consequently any triple \\((A,B,C)\\) satisfying (5) must obey  \n\n\\[\n|A|+|B|+|C|\\ge n . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\ns_{\\min}\\ge n . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Achieving the bound\n\nConsider the family of the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D:=\\{x+y=t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are of the third type in (3), hence none of them is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=a+b\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal D\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal D\\) satisfies (5) with  \n\n\\[\nA=\\varnothing,\\qquad B=\\varnothing,\\qquad C=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nand uses exactly \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  Consequently  \n\n\\[\ns_{\\min}=n . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Determination of the possible values of \\(k\\)\n\nFrom (9) we obtain the maximal possible number of sunny lines\n\n\\[\nk_{\\max}=n-s_{\\min}=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence for every \\(n\\ge 4\\) we must have \\(k=0\\).\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) consists of the three points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\nThe two diagonal lines \\(x+y=2\\) and \\(x+y=3\\) already cover all three points, so a covering with \\(s=2\\) non\u2011sunny lines exists.  Adding any sunny line (for instance \\(y=2x\\)) yields a configuration with exactly \\(k=1\\) sunny line.  Because of the lower bound (6) we cannot have \\(s\\le 1\\); therefore \\(k\\) cannot be \\(2\\) or \\(3\\).  Thus for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\begin{cases}\nk=0\\text{ or }k=1, & n=3,\\\\[2pt]\nk=0, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}\n}\n\\]\n\nThe construction for \\(k=0\\) is the family of the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=t\\;(2\\le t\\le n+1)\\).  \nFor \\(n=3,\\;k=1\\) we may take the two diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\;x+y=3\\) together with any sunny line (e.g. \\(y=2x\\)).  \n\nThese families satisfy all the requirements, and the lower\u2011bound argument shows that no other values of \\(k\\) are possible."
