 \\(n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 5.1 A base covering with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines  \n\nKeep the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) (non‑sunny).  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,n+1-i),\\qquad Q_i=(1,i),\\qquad \nL_i=\\text{the line through }P_i\\text{ and }Q_i .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\(Q_i\\) belongs to \\(S_n\\) because \\(1+i\\le n\\).  \nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{(n+1-i)-i}{i-1}= \\frac{n+1-2i}{i-1}.\n\\]\n\n*If \\(i=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) (possible only when \\(n\\) is odd) the numerator vanishes and the line would be horizontal; in that case we replace \\(Q_i\\) by \\((2,i-1)\\), which yields a non‑horizontal slope.*  \nIn all cases \\(m_i\\neq0,\\,-1,\\) and the line is not vertical; therefore each \\(L_i\\) is **sunny**.\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}\\cup\\{x+y=n+1\\}\n\\]  \n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nFor a sum \\(t\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the point \\((1,t-1)\\) lies on \\(L_{t-1}\\); hence every point of \\(S_n\\) whose sum is \\(t\\) is covered by \\(L_{t-1}\\).  \nAll points with sum \\(n+1\\) lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Thus every element of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 5.2 Reducing the number of sunny lines  \n\nIf we wish to have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), we keep the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) and the first \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).  \nFor each index \\(i\\) with \\(k<i\\le n-1\\) we replace the sunny line \\(L_i\\) by the diagonal line \\(x+y=i+1\\) (which is non‑sunny).  \nBecause the diagonal \\(x+y=i+1\\) already contains all points of sum \\(i+1\\), the covering property is preserved.  \n\nAfter the replacement we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, all distinct, and all points of \\(S_n\\) are still covered.\n\nConsequently for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,4\\) give a covering.  \n* \\(k=1\\): the two vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2\\) together with the sunny line \\(y=x\\) cover all six points; exactly one line is sunny.  \n* \\(k\\ge2\\): a sunny line can contain at most one point of the anti‑diagonal \\(D=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  With two or more sunny lines at most two points of \\(D\\) can be covered, leaving at least one point of \\(D\\) uncovered; the remaining lines (there are at most one non‑sunny line left) cannot cover the three points of \\(S_3\\) whose sum is different from \\(n+1\\).  Hence \\(k\\ge2\\) is impossible.\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7. Impossibility of all‑sunny configurations  \n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, each could contain at most one point of the anti‑diagonal \\(D\\); because \\(|D|=n\\), every sunny line would be forced to contain exactly one point of \\(D\\).  No line would remain to cover any point whose sum differs from \\(n+1\\), contradicting condition 1.  Therefore **\\(k=n\\) never occurs**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8. Final answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk=0\\text{ or }k=1, & n=3,\\\\[2pt]\nk=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThe constructions given in Sections 5.1–5.2 (for \\(n\\ge4\\)) and Section 6 (for \\(n=3\\)) prove that every listed value of \\(k\\) can indeed be realised, while the arguments of Sections 2, 3 and 7 show that no other value of \\(k\\) is possible."
