\\) is the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   Hence any covering must contain either  \n\n   * the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) (contributing one to \\(|C|\\)), **or**  \n   * at least \\(n\\) vertical/horizontal lines (contributing at least \\(n\\) to \\(|A|+|B|\\)).  \n\n   In both cases we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\ge n. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A covering with exactly \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n   \\]  \n\n   consists of \\(n\\) diagonal (non\u2011sunny) lines and clearly satisfies (2).  \n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   s_{\\min}=n. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **From the bound to the set of possible \\(k\\).**  \n   Equation (4) gives \\(k_{\\max}=n-s_{\\min}=0\\); i.e. **at least one non\u2011sunny line is unavoidable**.  \n   The construction in step\u202f3 shows that the bound is sharp, so the *minimal* number of non\u2011sunny lines is exactly \\(n\\).  \n   Consequently the *maximal* number of sunny lines equals \\(n-n=0\\) **when we insist that every non\u2011sunny line be of the diagonal type**.  \n   However, the argument above does **not** forbid using *other* non\u2011sunny lines (vertical or horizontal) together with sunny ones; it only tells us that we need at least \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines **if we do not use the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\)**.\n\n   The crucial observation is that the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) can be replaced by **any** collection of vertical/horizontal lines that together cover the set \\(D\\).  \n   The set \\(D\\) consists of \\(n\\) points with pairwise distinct \\(x\\)\u2011coordinates and pairwise distinct \\(y\\)\u2011coordinates, so it can be covered by exactly two vertical (or two horizontal) lines **iff** \\(n\\le 2\\), which never happens for \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   Hence **any covering of \\(S_n\\) must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines**.  \n   Combining this with (3) we obtain the sharper lower bound  \n\n   \\[\n   s_{\\min}=n\\quad\\text{for }n=3,\\qq