erage.*  \n   \u2013 Every point of \\(S_n\\) with sum \\(t\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) lies on the line \\(L_{t-1}\\) because that line contains the point \\((1,t-1)\\) whose sum is \\(t\\).  \n   \u2013 Every point with sum \\(n+1\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   Thus **all** points of \\(S_n\\) are covered.\n\n   *Step\u202f5.2 \u2013 obtaining fewer sunny lines.*  \n   The construction above gives a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   If we wish to have only \\(k\\) sunny lines with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), we simply replace any \\(n-1-k\\) of the sunny lines \\(L_i\\) by the remaining diagonal lines \\(x+y=t\\) (which are non\u2011sunny).  The replacement does not destroy the covering because each diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) already contains all points of sum \\(t\\).  After the replacement we still have \\(n\\) distinct lines, now with precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n   Consequently **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable for all \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n6. **The special case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n   \\]  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  The three diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,4\\) give a covering with no sunny line.  \n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Take the two vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2\\) (non\u2011sunny) and the sunny line \\(y=x\\).  The three lines are distinct and together cover all six points, so exactly one sunny line is possible.  \n\n   *\\(k\\ge2\\).*  Suppose at least two lines were sunny.  As observed in step\u202f1, a sunny line can contain at most one point of the anti\u2011diagonal \\(D=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  With two sunny lines we could cover at most two points of \\(D\\); the remaining point of \\(D\\) would have to be covered by a non\u2011sunny line, leaving at most one line left for the other three points of \\(S_3\\), which is impossible.  Hence \\(k\\ge2\\) cannot occur.\n\n   Therefore for \\(n=3\\) the only 